### 1. Architecture review

Two findings. Both verified by reading the code.

**[P1] (confidence: 9/10) `NavigationHistory.swift:86` + `AppStore.swift:3748` — once an entry carries content, tab-id is no longer a valid liveness key.**

Motivating lines:
```swift
// NavigationHistory.swift:86 — prune drops an entry when its tab id is gone
if tabs.contains(e.tab) { continue }
// AppStore.swift:3748 — isLive asks only whether the tab still exists
return terminals.tabs(for: target).contains { $0.id == loc.tab }
// TerminalSessions.swift:410-411 — content moves between tabs, so tab ≠ content
tab.content = desc.makeTabContent()
tabsByTarget[target.id]?[previewID] = tab
```
Replay can land recorded content in a *different* tab than the one recorded (the preview slot moves). That makes tab-id-keyed pruning asymmetric in a way you haven't seen yet: browse Gemfile → user.rb in preview tab P, Keep Open on P, browse routes.rb into new preview Q. The Gemfile entry names P. Closing **Q** prunes nothing; closing **P** erases the Gemfile step — even though both are equally replayable from the snapshot. Same browse run, different survival, depending on which tab you later pinned.

**[P2] (confidence: 10/10) `TerminalSessions.swift:383-389` — the existing ASCII diagram goes stale, and the new replay algorithm has none.**

**[P2] (confidence: 10/10) `TerminalSessions.swift:383-389` — existing diagram goes stale; new replay algorithm has none.**

The diagram that documents the content openers:
```
//   single-click file ─▶ openDiffPreview ─┬─ persisted tab for this file+rev exists → focus it (Inv C)
//                                          ├─ a preview tab exists → retarget IN PLACE, same id (Inv B)
//                                          └─ else → new preview tab           (≤1 preview/target: Inv A)
```
After this change every one of those arrows also records a history step. Your rule is that diagram maintenance ships in the same commit.

### 2. Code quality review

One finding.

**[P1] (confidence: 9/10) `NavigationHistory.swift` (new `NavContent.==`) — a `default:` arm in the equality switch silently un-fixes this bug for the next content kind.**

Motivating lines — the identity rules `==` must respect, and the protocol that does *not* cover them:
```swift
// DiffDescriptor.swift:37-39
func sameFile(as other: DiffDescriptor) -> Bool { path == other.path && source == other.source }
// ChangesetDescriptor.swift:24
func sameChangeset(as other: ChangesetDescriptor) -> Bool { commitID == other.commitID }
// TerminalSessions.swift:136 — the only protocol requirement; no `sameX` in it
func matches(_ content: TabContent) -> Bool
```
`TabContent` (`TerminalSessions.swift:101`) earns its "closed set — adding a kind is a compiler-guided change" claim because every switch over it is exhaustive. A `switch (lhs, rhs)` with `default: return false` breaks that: add a fifth kind and `.snippet == .snippet` returns `false` forever, so every snippet click records a step and dedup never collapses. That is this bug's failure mode wearing a new hat — a new content kind not participating in history.

